 ## `analysis`
 
+### `allow-out-of-order-references`
+
+Whether ty should tolerate references to names that are only bound later in the same
+scope or in an enclosing lexical scope.
+
+By default, ty reports `unresolved-reference` for a load whose first binding is reachable
+only after the load, mirroring Python's runtime behavior:
+
+```python
+total = offset + base  # error: Name `base` used when not defined
+base = 1
+offset = 2
+```
+
+When enabled, ty considers bindings that are reachable anywhere in the lexical scope
+chain before giving up on a load, so the example above type checks. Names that are never
+bound in a scope the load could see (such as a misspelled identifier) still produce
+`unresolved-reference`.
+
+Note that types are still inferred from the bindings that actually reach the load, so a
+forward reference can resolve to a type that differs from what Python observes at
+runtime. Defaults to `false`.
+
+**Default value**: `false`
+
+**Type**: `bool`
+
+**Example usage**:
+
+=== "pyproject.toml"
+
+    ```toml
+    [tool.ty.analysis]
+    # Do not report forward references at module scope
+    allow-out-of-order-references = true
+    ```
+
+=== "ty.toml"
+
+    ```toml
+    [analysis]
+    # Do not report forward references at module scope
+    allow-out-of-order-references = true
+    ```
+
+---
+
 ### `allowed-unresolved-imports`
 
 A list of module glob patterns for which `unresolved-import` diagnostics should be suppressed.
